- **racy entries:** an entry whose mtime is not safely older than the moment the cache was written (git's "racily clean" case) is always re-read, so a file changed within the same timestamp tick is never missed.

**Done when:**
- the spec's repository layout and cache contract list the new cache, with the racy rule;
- e2e: unchanged files are not re-read; an edit that keeps the size is still found; an edit in the same second as the cache write is still found; deleting the cache changes nothing but time;
- `lap status` on the typec repository, with nothing changed, measured before and after (target: well under 1 s), in the closing comment.

`lap status` compares every tracked file with its shadow on every call: 3.75 s on the typec repository (E-11). Almost every file is unchanged each time.

Add a stat cache, the way git's index does it: for each tracked file, the size and mtime (with nanoseconds where the platform has them) it had when last found equal to its shadow. `status` (and `commit`'s detection, if it walks more than the one file) reads a file only when its size or mtime differs from the cached values.

It is a cache under the cache contract:
- a new file in `.lap/`; `log.jsonl` is not touched, and nothing new is committed (the repository ignores everything in `.lap/` but the log);
- deleting it changes speed only: the next call compares everything and rebuilds it;
- written by writers under the lock; a reader that finds it missing or stale falls back to full comparison and does not write it — or, if readers must stay write-free, `status` only uses it and writers keep it current. Decide which, in the spec.
- **racy entries:** an entry whose mtime is not safely older than the moment the cache was written (git's "racily clean" case) is always re-read, so a file changed within the same timestamp tick is never missed.

**Done when:**
- the spec's repository layout and cache contract list the new cache, with the racy rule;
- e2e: unchanged files are not re-read; an edit that keeps the size is still found; an edit in the same second as the cache write is still found; deleting the cache changes nothing but time;
