On the user's Windows checkout of typec, Git had written most files with CRLF while the lap log (recorded on macOS) holds LF, so `lap status` showed ~14,000 files as entirely rewritten. The SPEC says "CRLF is preserved byte-faithfully", so lap compared bytes.

Decided with the user: do the storing half of what git does. lap never writes the working tree, so there is no checkout half.
- When lap reads a text file it treats `\r\n` as `\n`: the log and the shadows hold LF.
- When comparing, a trailing `\r` on a shadow or log line is ignored too, so a log that already holds CRLF lines does not churn.
- A change of line endings alone is not a change to lap. lap does not read `.gitattributes` or `core.autocrlf`.

**Done when:**
- SPEC "Edit detection" says lap records text with LF endings, replacing "CRLF is preserved byte-faithfully";
- a CRLF working copy of LF-recorded files is clean; an edit in it is one edit, recorded with LF;
- e2e covers both, and any existing CRLF test is updated to the new rule;
- lap's suites pass.

